    <h1>Raven in Native American Tradition</h1>
    <p class="id-subtitle">Creator, trickster, and transformer — an overview with boundaries respected</p>
  </header>

  <section class="id-snippet" aria-label="Quick answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> In many Pacific Northwest and sub-Arctic Indigenous traditions, Raven is a <strong>creator-trickster figure</strong> — the being who brought light into the world and transformed existence through cunning and deception. Raven stories vary significantly across nations; there is no single unified "Native American raven tradition." This page provides a broad overview. For specific traditions, the right primary sources are nation-specific: Haida, Tlingit, Tsimshian, and others have distinct Raven cycles.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Important Framing Note</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> "Native American tradition" is not a single thing — it encompasses hundreds of distinct nations with distinct languages, cultures, and oral traditions. This page covers a general theme (raven as trickster-creator in Northwest and sub-Arctic cultures) with specific examples from documented written sources. It does not represent the full diversity of Indigenous raven traditions, and it does not speak to any living tradition's current beliefs, ceremonies, or spiritual practices. For accurate knowledge of a specific nation's tradition, that nation's own cultural resources and scholarship are the appropriate source.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>The Pacific Northwest Coast Raven Cycle</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> Across the Pacific Northwest Coast — in traditions of the Haida (Haida Gwaii), Tlingit (Alaska and British Columbia), Tsimshian, Gitxsan, Heiltsuk, Kwakwaka'wakw, and neighbouring peoples — Raven appears as a central culture-hero and trickster:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Raven Steals the Light:</strong> One of the most widely documented Raven stories involves Raven obtaining light (often held in a box by a chief or supernatural being) and releasing it into the world. This story exists in many versions across Pacific Northwest nations — often with Raven transforming himself (into a pine needle, a human child) to gain access to the box.</li>
      <li><strong>Raven as transformer:</strong> Raven reshapes the world, creating rivers, mountains, fish, and essential features of the landscape through tricks, deceptions, and transformations.</li>
      <li><strong>Moral ambiguity:</strong> Raven is neither hero nor villain in a Western sense — he is greedy, selfish, clever, and transformative. Good outcomes often arise from selfish motives.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The documented stories are complex and cannot be summarised as simple "lessons." They are part of living oral traditions held by specific nations.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a>,<a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Sub-Arctic and Alaska Traditions</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> Raven also appears prominently in traditions of Yup'ik, Inupiat, and other sub-Arctic peoples:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>In Yup'ik and Inupiat traditions, Raven (Tulugaq) is associated with creation and transformation, with stories parallel to but distinct from Pacific Northwest versions</li>
      <li>Raven's role as a scavenger and survivor in Arctic environments made him a natural figure for stories about cunning, adaptability, and the acquisition of resources</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Again: the specific content and cultural context of these stories belong to the nations that hold them, and this overview is not a substitute for those sources.<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>The Ecological Foundation</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Raven's role as a trickster-creator in Pacific Northwest traditions has an obvious ecological foundation: Common Ravens are highly visible, cognitively complex, opportunistic, and associated with both life (following hunters to camps, consuming their leavings) and death (at carcasses). Heinrich (2000) notes that the raven's real-world behaviour — apparent planning, deception, manipulation of other species — provides a natural basis for trickster mythology across cultures that live in close contact with the bird.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Recommended Nation-Specific Sources</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> For those who want accurate understanding of specific traditions, rather than a generalised overview:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Haida:</strong> Works by Haida scholars and artists including Bill Reid and Robert Bringhurst's collaborative <em>The Raven Steals the Light</em> (1984, Haida stories retold with Haida storyteller participation) — though this is a retelling, not a primary source</li>
      <li><strong>Tlingit:</strong> Swanton, J. R. (1909). <em>Tlingit Myths and Texts</em>. Bureau of American Ethnology — an early collected source; more recent Tlingit-authored scholarship exists</li>
      <li><strong>Nation websites and cultural centres</strong> are increasingly the best resources for accurate, current information about specific traditions</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="id-faq id-section" aria-labelledby="faq-h2">
    <h2 id="faq-h2">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    {faq.map(item => (
